{
  "id": "p19",
  "title": "Units",
  "lessons": [
    {
      "id": "p19-l1",
      "title": "A Unit of Your Own",
      "xp": 15,
      "intro": [
        "Turbo Pascal 4 (1987) brought units: a unit is a file of ready-made routines that any program can use. You already know one: crt. With uses Maths; a program gets everything the unit Maths offers.",
        "A unit has two parts. The interface lists what others may use, with only the headings of the routines. The implementation has the full routines. It ends with end. like a program.",
        "Turbo Pascal compiled each unit once into a .TPU file, so big programs compiled much faster. Here, write the unit above the program in the same editor."
      ],
      "example": {
        "code": [
          "unit Maths;",
          "",
          "interface",
          "",
          "function Square(x: integer): integer;",
          "function IsPrime(n: integer): boolean;",
          "",
          "implementation",
          "",
          "function Square(x: integer): integer;",
          "begin",
          "  Square := x * x;",
          "end;",
          "",
          "function IsPrime(n: integer): boolean;",
          "var",
          "  d: integer;",
          "begin",
          "  IsPrime := n > 1;",
          "  for d := 2 to n - 1 do",
          "    if n mod d = 0 then IsPrime := false;",
          "end;",
          "",
          "end.",
          "",
          "program Primes;",
          "uses Maths;",
          "var",
          "  i: integer;",
          "begin",
          "  for i := 1 to 10 do",
          "    if IsPrime(i) then",
          "      writeln(i:2, Square(i):5);",
          "end."
        ],
        "output": [
          " 2    4",
          " 3    9",
          " 5   25",
          " 7   49"
        ]
      },
      "exercises": [
        {
          "id": "p19-l1-e1",
          "type": "multiple_choice",
          "prompt": "What is a unit?",
          "options": [
            "A file of routines that programs can use with uses",
